  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nToday into tonight...\n\nOur area remains on the eastern periphery of a large 596dam high \nas an upper disturbance is expected to drop into our northeast \nzones later this evening into tonight. Prior to more widespread \nstorm development, temperatures across the area will warm into the\nmid 90s F with upper 90s F near and west of I-55. With continued \nhigh humidity, heat stress is expected to increase today. In the \nnortheast where peak heat index values will be around 105 F where \na \"Limited\" threat area for dangerous heat remains advertised. \nElsewhere, an \"Elevated\" threat for dangerous heat will be remains\nfor heat indices between 105-110 F. As the evening disturbance \ndives southeastward into our area from the northwest, it will \nencounter this unstable, hot and humid environment. Sufficient \ndeep layer flow enhanced by organized thunderstorm outflows will \nlikely allow for some of this convection to become strong to \nsevere with damaging wind gusts being the main threat. /22/86/ \n\nThursday into next week...\n\nThe ongoing northwest flow pattern across the region will persist\nthrough the end of the work week. This will continue to provide \ndecent rain chances across the forecast area as disturbances \nembedded in this flow continue to move across the region. \n\nThunderstorms from the previous overnight should be diminishing into\nearly Thursday morning, as instability likewise weakens. Chances \nfor showers and storms will remain in the forecast Thursday \nafternoon and again on Friday, especially across locations along \nand southeast of the Natchez Trace Parkway.\n\nOver the weekend and into early next week, high pressure aloft will \nbegin to re-assert itself across the Lower Mississippi River Valley. \nThis will result in lessening rain chances across the forecast area. \nUnfortunately, the trade-off will again be increasing heat. By \nnext Monday, some need for at least a \"Limited\" threat of \nincreased heat stress will likely be required across portions of \nthe forecast area with higher outlooks possible. /19/86/",
